/** YD001 quality-pass translations siman 105 — Part D: siftei-kohen, beur-hagra, kaf-hachayim */
export const PART_D = {
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] Forbidden food that fell with permitted food for twenty-four hours in cold is called pickled and behold it is as cooked, etc. Regarding vessels — whether we say pickled is like cooked, i.e. if forbidden food was steeped in a permitted vessel for the measure of pickling which is twenty-four hours, whether the vessel becomes forbidden — there is a dispute. Ohr HaChaim in his compendium general rule 37 end law 1 wrote that pickling does not forbid except sixty against the peel; and in his compendium general rule 30 law 9 wrote that also in vessels there is pickling and if forbidden food was steeped in it for twenty-four hours the entire vessel becomes forbidden; and likewise Tosafot Chadashim end of general rule 5 wrote this, and it is not as Ohr HaChaim wrote in general rule 37 that pickling does not forbid in a vessel except peel. Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahat Turei Zahav section 6, Lechem HaPanim section 1, Mishnat Zahav section 1, Arukh HaShulchan section 1, Chokhmat Adam general rule 57 section 11. And regarding what we say later siman 135 seif 13 regarding yayin nesech that even when putting it away for storage it does not absorb more than a peel — Ohr HaChaim already answered this there and wrote that for yayin nesech they were lenient, see there. And likewise Mishnat Zahav there in the name of Shach that one should not learn from yayin nesech, see there. And as is clear from the words of the other Acharonim mentioned that one should not learn from yayin nesech who hold like Ohr HaChaim. However Taz s.k. 1 wrote to challenge Ohr HaChaim's words mentioned and concluded that after the fact one should not forbid them through forbidden food that fell for twenty-four hours; and likewise Mishnat Zahav there end of section 1 that he found a manuscript for Taz that pickled does not absorb in a vessel except peel and there is sixty in the food against the peel — end of his words — and it appears he learned this from the law of yayin nesech. And likewise is Shach's view at end of s.k. 2 who wrote that also otherwise there is an opinion that it is not called pickled except in three days etc. — it appears he does not hold like Ohr HaChaim in this. And likewise Lechem Pri Chadash in seif 1 that according to Bach and Shach one does not forbid after the fact. However from Shach's words later siman 135 s.k. 33 and siman 137 s.k. 9 it appears explicitly that he holds there is pickling in vessels, see there. And if so what is written here is not precise. It turns out this law is a twofold dispute whether we learn from yayin nesech and peel suffices or not. And see further Rabbi Chakal Yisrael Chayei David part 1 siman 87 and Orach Chayim what he expanded at length on this matter. He brought the views of those who forbid and those who permit, see there; and therefore it appears that in great loss one may rely on those who permit — that is if forbidden food was steeped in a vessel for twenty-four hours and before he has his own twenty-four hours of steeping in it a permitted food, one may be lenient only in great loss but not in minor loss since many forbade.`,
    '2#_': `(2) And what is the case — in a wooden vessel or earthenware vessel one should forbid, but in a metal vessel one should not forbid if forbidden food fell for twenty-four hours. Ohr HaChaim there. However Minchat Yosef on Tosafot Chadashim there section 12 wrote that there is no distinction between wooden, earthenware, or metal vessels regarding pickling. And likewise Beit Levi section 1; but Peleti end of section 1 brought support for Ohr HaChaim's words and likewise Arukh HaShulchan there that the main view is as Ohr HaChaim's words. And in a glass vessel one who is lenient in great loss did not lose. Mishnat Zahav there end of the words "and if". And this is according to his view that he forbids pickling of vessels in every case; but according to what was written in the previous section that in great loss one may be lenient there is no difference, and all the more so in metal vessels and glass vessels where there is a dispute.`,
    '3#_': `(3) And likewise a vessel of forbidden food that is not absorbed from the beginning that permitted food was steeped in it for twenty-four hours — the whole thing is forbidden. Ohr HaChaim general rule 30 law 9. But Bach s.k. 1 challenged this — why should the permitted food be forbidden in a vessel of forbidden food that is not absorbed from the beginning, since the prohibition is from pickling and behold at that moment it is not absorbed from the beginning and the vessel has no power to forbid the permitted food steeped in it, see there; and likewise Shach challenged s.k. 2. And Kelalei HaGahot in Hagahat Turei Zahav section 6 wrote that for practical halachah we rule like Taz and Shach that permitted food steeped in a vessel of forbidden food that is not absorbed from the beginning, or milk in a meat vessel or vice versa — the food is not forbidden because it is nosen taam lifgam and permitted after the fact. Beit Levi section 1, Chokhmat Adam section 3, Chakal Yisrael there. However Peri Chadash section 1 agreed with Ohr HaChaim. And likewise Lechem Pri Chadash section 1, Kereti section 1. It turns out this matter is a twofold dispute and therefore one should not permit after the fact except in great loss; and what Zivchei Tzedek section 2 wrote to permit even in minor loss and he brought support that this is Shulchan Aruch's view that there is no pickling for vessels since he ruled regarding yayin nesech that there is no pickling for vessels — we already wrote above in section 1 that regarding yayin nesech all agree except whether we learn from yayin nesech or not; and if so from Shulchan Aruch's words one cannot decide that he holds that in every case there is no pickling for vessels; and if so we return to our law that this matter is a twofold dispute and one should not permit except in great loss. And see above siman 69 section 33.`,
    '4#_': `(4) Also in a cooked thing there is the law of pickled, and not like Rabbi Pri Tzadik siman 19. Arukh HaShulchan section 2; and this is such as if cooked forbidden food fell into a moist dish such as one that has gravy and it fell for twenty-four hours — the dish is forbidden because pickled is like cooked. And see later siman 106 section 15.`,
    '5#_': `(5) Also for one who holds there is pickling for vessels and the vessel is forbidden — and if it is a metal vessel it requires hagalah; nevertheless if it is an earthenware vessel one does not need breaking since although an earthenware vessel never leaves its absorption, this is where it absorbed through heat; but if it absorbed through pickling which is in cold, filling with water three times for twenty-four hours or hagalah three times suffices. Chakal Yisrael there siman 87. And likewise Arukh HaShulchan later siman 135 section 14 in the name of several poskim that an earthenware vessel in other prohibitions that absorbed through cold — filling and pouring help, and not like Shach there s.k. 33 who wrote that filling and pouring do not help except for wine or regarding chametz on Pesach and not for other prohibitions unless it is not absorbed from the beginning, see there. Nevertheless initially if possible one should be concerned for Shach's words and with him one should not do pouring for an earthenware vessel in other prohibitions except after twenty-four hours.`,
    '6#_': `(6) There. Forbidden food that fell with permitted food for twenty-four hours in cold is called pickled, etc. And if the pickling was in stages — such as forbidden food fell with permitted food for twelve hours and they removed the permitted food outside and after some hours returned and placed it into the forbidden food for one hour — whether it is considered pickled in such a case where there was an interruption in between or not, see above siman 70 section 242 where we brought the dispute in this and wrote there that the consensus of most poskim is to permit after the fact if it already happened, and even one who wants to be stringent nevertheless in great loss it is permitted, see there.`,
    '7#_': `(7) And pickling applies only to a solid thing in a liquid thing — one emits and absorbs from this to that, not in two moist things that would immediately mix and forbid each other, and not in two solid things even if they are somewhat moist — pickling does not apply to them even if they stand many days, thus. And even fat and forbidden food placed inside it — as long as it is hard it is not called pickled; only if it is soft as stated above siman 104 in hagahah; but if it is soft that the forbidden food placed inside it moves from place to place it is called pickled. And forbidden or permitted food pickled in cold water and the like — Minchat Yosef on Tosafot Chadashim in responsum at end of the book siman 14 permitted and said that in cold pickling does not apply and likewise it is reasonable that it does not move and all the more so becomes like a dry stone inside it and does not emit; but Beit Levi in responsum at end of the book siman 29 and here section 1 wrote in the name of Bach that it is pickled and I did not understand — for whatever is hard pickling does not apply and such is the halachah at least in great loss. And dry with dry that are somewhat moist — even sealing does not require after the fact and nevertheless initially one should take like the thickness of a finger. Mishnat Zahav section 1 in the name of Beit Yosef Chayei Adam part 1 section 1 and in Ein Mishpat there sections 3 and 4, Arukh HaShulchan section 8; and likewise Chokhmat Adam wrote this, general rule 58 section 1.`,
    '8#_': `(8) There hagahah. And wherever we say pickled is like cooked — even what is outside the pickling is forbidden, etc. And there are those who are lenient regarding what is outside. And likewise in Tosafot Chadashim general rule 22 law 6. Taz s.k. 2. Shach s.k. 1. Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahat Turei Zahav section 4, Peri Chadash section 2, Peri Toar section 2, Lechem Pri Chadash section 2, Beit Levi section 2, Chokhmat Adam section 4, Shulchan Petach David section 1, Chokhmat Adam there. And even in forbidden oil — in salting what is outside the brine is permitted in pickling. Kenesset HaGedolah there. Minchat Yosef on Tosafot Chadashim there or Taz. Beit Levi there. But Shach there wrote that this is when the forbidden food is lean — but if the forbidden food is oil even what is outside the brine is forbidden. And likewise Peri Chadash there that if the forbidden piece is oil even what is outside the brine is forbidden for all — because the fat spreads. Kereti section 2, Chokhmat Adam there. Beit Yosef there section 4. However Arukh HaShulchan end of section 14 wrote that from Rama's wording it does not appear so but certainly in pickling for twenty-four hours also in oil it does not spread upward. And Shulchan Petach David there wrote that when there is no great loss one should forbid in oil like Shach in pickling for twenty-four hours. And such is the practical law since there is a dispute one should not be lenient except in great loss. And this is if there is not sixty in the pickling against the forbidden food — the permitted food that is outside is permitted in great loss; but if there is sixty in the permitted food inside the pickling against the forbidden food, otherwise everything is permitted even what is inside the pickling without great loss and all the more so what is outside.`,
    '9#_': `(9) However from piece to piece that is placed one on the other it appears that even Shach would agree that in oil outside the pickling it does not emit from this to that. Shulchan Petach David there end of section 1 — meaning even for Shach who forbids in oil what is outside the pickling — if there is a piece on that piece from which emission goes outside the pickling, that piece is permitted and this is even without great loss.`,
    '10#_': `(10) And in pickling in brine and in vinegar it is not inferior to salting — we are stringent in every case. Arukh HaShulchan there. Meaning even for those who permit in oil what is outside the pickling — if it was pickled in brine or vinegar one should forbid what is outside the pickling because they are sharp things and spread upward.`,
    '11#_': `(11) And all this is in pickled that is cold; but in cooked — such as if a forbidden piece fell into a permitted dish and part of the piece is outside the gravy — we require sixty against the entire piece even what is outside the gravy because through the gravy that is boiling it emits and absorbs in all of it. Ohr HaChaim general rule 28 end of section 3, Chokhmat Adam in its explanation section 70; and likewise above siman 94 section 2, see there.`,
    '12#_': `(12) And if the gravy is forbidden and a permitted piece fell into it and part of it is outside the gravy — we require sixty in the permitted piece that is inside the gravy and that part that is outside the gravy does not combine for sixty to nullify the forbidden food — we go to stringency as stated above siman 92 section 5, see there.`,
    '13#_': `(13) There in hagahah. And doubt of pickled is forbidden. Because pickled is forbidden from the Torah. Ohr HaChaim general rule 30 end law 1, Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahat Turei Zahav section 3 and challenged Bach s.k. 3 who disagrees and likewise Shach in Nekudat HaKesef challenged him. Peri Chadash section 3, Peri Toar section 3, Lechem Pri Chadash section 3, Beit Levi section 3, Kereti section 3, Mishnat Zahav section 1 in the words "and pickling", Arukh HaShulchan section 4, Chokhmat Adam general rule 58 section 4, Beit Yosef Chayei Adam part 1 section 2.`,
    '14#_': `(14) There in hagahah. And doubt of pickled is forbidden. Meaning we see it was pickled only that there is doubt whether it was pickled a full day or not. Taz s.k. 3. Lechem Pri Chadash there. Beit Levi there. Mishnat Zahav section 3.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Forbidden food that fell off, etc. From what is said in chapter 3 of Pesachim (daf 44 side 2) and chapter 6 of Nazir — what is its novelty, etc. — that if you roast it all day [and Rashi on alma explains one cannot say an actual day, for there is a long day and a short day, and your words would need measures] — it implies that other prohibitions are forbidden d'oraisa from something falling off alma — Rosh chapter 2 and chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah:`,
    '1#ב': `In cold. Meaning even in twenty-four hours, not like Rashi who wrote that it is not called kavush except in vinegar; and proof from what is said end of chapter 7 of Shevi'it and new rose, etc., and chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah {Rama: (daf 68 side 2)} that mouse, etc., and as is said Tosafot there s.v. that, etc.: (collected) forbidden, etc. Not like Rashi who wrote kavush specifically in vinegar, for we learned rose new, etc. Mordechai end of chapter 2 Yevamot (end of his words): (collected) forbidden, etc. Not like Rashi who wrote specifically in vinegar; and so Beit Yosef explains kavush in vinegar — and it is not so, for in the mishnah end of chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah daf 39 side 2 and pickled things that are not their way, etc., and likewise in the mishnah daf 36 side 2 and pickled things that are their way, etc., and they said in the Gemara daf 38 side 2 but it is known, etc. (end of his words):`,
    '1#ג': `Kavush and behold, etc. Chapter 25 {Rama: (daf 76 side 1)} and chapter 95 and chapter 105:`,
    '1#ד': `And the whole thing becomes forbidden. As is said in chapter 95 daf 97 side 2 what is your reasoning, etc., and Tosafot what, etc.:`,
    '1#ה': `But less, etc. As is said chapter 25 {Rama: (there)} and chapter 105 {Rama: (daf 107 side 2):}`,
    '1#ו': `And wherever we say, etc. Like cooked as is said in chapter 25 daf 108 a drop of milk, etc., and Tosafot there s.v. a drop, etc.:`,
    '1#ז': `And some are lenient. That we do not say kavush kemevushal except what is actually kavush:`,
    '1#ח': `And doubt, etc. From what is said that if you roast it all day, etc., as above — from which it implies forbidden in other prohibitions from the Torah; but in basar b'chalav permitted from the Torah and as is said in Chullin daf 108 side 1 the way of cooking, etc., and therefore he said only, etc., and nevertheless d'rabbanan forbidden as we say {Rama: (there in the mishnah daf 116)} one who curdles with hide of, etc.:`,
    '1#ט': `And if it is, etc. So too Rosh in chapter 25 end of seif 49 in brine and his reason: since it is like cooked we estimate by the measure of cooking; and as the Geonim said the measure of salting is like the measure of roasting — from what is said {Rama: (Chullin daf 97 side 2)} salted like boiling that is roasted. And Beit Yosef learned the same for vinegar [and as is said in chapter 1 of Maaser and chapter 3 of Shabbos R' Yehudah, etc., vinegar and brine — behold they are equivalent] — but Rosh in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah seif 11 wrote that in vinegar too we require me'eit l'eit and specifically brine he wrote thus because salt in it boils as is said salted like boiling; and in Yerushalmi chapter 1 of Maaser halachah 6 side 4 R' Yehudah salted like wine brine like vinegar — behold even for R' Yehudah vinegar like wine and wine therefore requires me'eit l'eit: (collected) and if it is, etc. On what was written above siman 135 s.k. 42 in hagahah {Rama: (in s.k. 42)} that even in its preparation Rashba holds thus and he learned from what is said in Avodah Zarah daf 33 side 2 now brine, etc. — but Rosh's view there is not so (end of his words):`,
    '1#י': `And in less, etc. So too Rosh in chapter 25 there, meaning from the law of salted like boiling that is roasted, and one needs only a peel as stated above in the section. And this certainly even according to Shulchan Aruch applies only to brine and it implies even immediately; but Ran's view is that it is not called salted except when there is the measure of salting as is said in siman 91 section 5:`,
    '1#כ': `And see above, etc. For there end of the siman he wrote however what is inside the brine, etc., and as will be explained:`,
    '2#א': `Heat, etc. As is said in chapter 3 of Shabbos {Rama: (daf 42)} the pot, etc., but, etc., and in the Gemara there in the baraita in a kli rishon it cooked, etc., and there in the baraita in a bathtub but in a cup, etc., and there {Rama: (daf 40 side 2)} take in kli sheni and put; and in end of chapter 8 of Chullin daf 104 side 2 in summary kli sheni is, etc., only, etc.:`,
    '2#ב': `And some say also, etc. And as is said in Yerushalmi chapter 3 of Shabbos and chapter 1 of Maaser halachah 6 until where R' Yehudah bar Pazi R' Simon in the name of Rabbah bar bar Chanah — until that the hand may be put in it and it scalds — all agree in kli sheni that it is permitted; what is the difference between kli rishon and kli sheni? R' Yehudah bar Bon said: here the hand rules and here the hand does not rule. R' Yonah said: here and here the hand does not rule — rather they made a distance for kli rishon {Rama: (see Orach Chayyim siman 318 section 9)} and did not make a distance for kli sheni. R' Mana said: this law is supported by the rice pot and the groats pot supports father — that pot of groats that you carry from place to place until it boils. Meaning a bowl of rice and of groats which is kli sheni and it boils and it supports R' Yonah that yad soledes bo in kli sheni and nevertheless it does not cook. What if one puts spices below and pours over them from above? R' Yonah said forbidden — pouring is like kli rishon; R' Yonah's leniency is from this: whether one cooked or poured boiling into it. R' Yosi said: there earthenware absorbs — spices are not cooked. Meaning: nevertheless pouring is like kli sheni and there the prohibition is because of absorption and earthenware absorbs even in kli sheni — otherwise for Shabbos whose prohibition is cooking, R' Yehudah bar Bon taught and the baraita taught also in a copper vessel — you may say copper absorbs. Meaning copper certainly does not absorb in kli sheni — rather we learn pouring is like kli rishon. What if one pours with boiling? R' Chanina son of R' Hillel said: dispute of R' Yonah and R' Yosi — meaning it also depends on the dispute above. R' Yitzchak bar Nafcha asked R' Mana: if one did so on Shabbos is he liable for cooking? If in kli rishon is he liable for cooking? Meaning in pouring. He said to him: as R' Zeira said — what is clear broth? Whatever the fire passes beneath it; and here what is clear food? Whatever the fire passes beneath it — meaning but in pouring, even though it is like kli sheni, it is only that it absorbs and emits as is said in Zevachim daf 95 side 2 absorbs without cooking, etc. From all this it implies kli sheni also does not absorb; and also proved from the Yerushalmi above that only kli rishon where yad soledes bo; and so they said in chapter 3 of Shabbos {Rama: (daf 40 side 2)} one water and one oil, etc., and Tosafot there and therefore he said that the hand, etc., and he said and forbids the whole thing as above from that a drop of milk, etc., and he said kli rishon — meaning even if not on the fire as is said in Shabbos there the pot {Rama: (daf 42)} the kettle he emptied, etc. {Rama: (daf 41)} and many places there; and even though in the Yerushalmi above they said whatever the fire passes beneath it — meaning in a vessel where the fire had passed beneath it {Rama: (not like Ramban end of Avodah Zarah who strained in chapter Yerushalmi)} and so in Chullin daf 104 side 2 lest it rise in the first pot: (collected) and some say, etc. Rashba's proofs from salting that absorbs kli rishon are no proof, for salting is different since it is like boiling that is roasted, and one might think even boiling that is cooked forbids the whole thing as is said daf 97 side 2; and his proof from what is said daf 8 side 2 do not scrape like a shovel, etc. — those mean lest milk remain on it and become salted or roasted with it; and his proof from what is said {Rama: (there)} on a knife — a knife is different because of the strain of the knife that absorbs kli rishon even in kli sheni according to his view, and as is said in siman 94 section 7 in hagahah and as stated above even according to those who say cold, etc. (end of his words):`,
    '2#ג': `And some say, etc. From what is said in chapter 25 daf 111 side 2 a bowl in which one salted, etc., and chapter 7 of Shabbos {Rama: (daf 75 side 2)} this one who salted in brine, etc., and if for cooking all exempt — behold even though it does not cook it absorbs; and at end of Avodah Zarah one who buys a utensil, etc., that was used in hot things and it implies even in kli sheni and basar b'chalav {Rama: (two more proofs):} (collected) and some say nevertheless, etc. And the Yerushalmi above Ramban explained and requires study: what he learned from kodshim that the Torah required pouring and rinsing and hagalah does not suffice for kli sheni — behold it absorbs much like kli rishon; but it absorbs even kli sheni and even copper and requires hagalah; and so in Orach Chayyim siman 451 section 6 (end of his words):`,
    '2#ד': `As much as a peel. Like heat of the slaughterhouse as we say in chapter 1 (daf 8 side 2) that it peels; and like salting and even in pouring it forbids only a peel as Tosafot said {Rama: (in Shabbos daf 42 and conclusion)} from that chapter 25 {Rama: (daf 76 side 1)} hot into cold, etc.:`,
    '3#א': `Everything forbidden. Will be explained in the section when; and what he said everything forbidden — so in the Gemara there hot into cold peels — from which in this the whole thing is forbidden:`,
    '3#ב': `And all this, etc. Also Maharshal wrote that in a solid item there is no distinction between kli rishon and kli sheni; and Tosafot Shabbos daf 40 side 2 s.v. and conclusion, etc., and one might say, etc., unlike a solid item; and his proof from fish that rose in a bowl and in the matter he disagreed and wrote that fish that rose in a bowl is like hot into cold since they were not yet in kli sheni — unlike when already in kli sheni; and proof from what is said at beginning of chapter 25 end end kli sheni is, etc., and there meat and cheese is: (collected) and all this, etc. Even though it is a solid item — not like Maharshal; and proof from what is said at beginning of Maasrot and one puts to sour — Rashi explained hot cake taken from the oven, etc., and he considered it kli sheni like porridge — see there (end of his words):`,
    '3#ג': `And if he left them, etc. If, etc. Not like Semak who wrote in siman 200 and one might say what they said in Zevachim daf 97 side 1 one piece touched a piece, etc., and perhaps one might say there it deals with this on this [and so Mordechai siman 763] and so the questioner to Rashba in Perishah — but Rashba answered him there is no distinction; and proof from chapter 25 daf 112 side 2 in the incident of Rav Mari that they did not ask whether they were this on that or next to each other — and it implies he forbade him everything since he did not specify a peel; and this is his approach below in section 11; and that of Zevachim is with weak roasting as is said in the section:`,
    '3#ד': `And if the one, etc. Like hot into cold since tattei does not apply:`,
    '3#ה': `Forbidden, etc. As is said in chapter 25 there we learned a drop fell from its gravy, etc., and Tosafot Chullin daf 111 side 2 s.v. the halachah, etc. And all this in oil prohibition according to Shulchan Aruch and in his approach everything is forbidden and therefore he was general:`,
    '3#ו': `Forbidden to pour, etc. As is said in chapter 5 of Machshirin one pours, etc., from cold to them from water — and the same for prohibition; and in chapter Maharai seif 103 he contradicted his words and said specifically regarding impurity and so for yayin nesech that depends on contact — unlike other prohibitions; and behold even in a metal vessel part of it hot, etc., and nevertheless they permit as stated above in siman 94 and therefore he said and b'dieved, etc.:`,
    '4#א': `In the baraita, etc. As is said in chapter 95 {Rama: (daf 96 side 2)} only that it was cooked, etc., and therefore he said therefore the thigh, etc.:`,
    '4#ב': `As much as removal. As is said in chapter 25 a drop fell from its gravy onto the earthenware and in the earthenware it boils as is said in the Gemara. There:`,
    '4#ג': `That it is like the thickness, etc. So Rosh wrote there:`,
    '4#ד': `Or a piece, etc. From that of Zevachim above and therefore he said and likewise from, etc., and as is said in the mishnah there:`,
    '4#ה': `And likewise if it fell, etc. For whatever is without gravy it does not spread and it is like roasted; and as stated daf 108 side 2 if you say do not shake at all, etc., and that piece is forbidden because milk spreads — and forbids even in roasting as is said in section 5 and as Tosafot said there side 1 s.v. a drop and see above section 7 as stated:`,
    '4#ו': `For Rashi, etc. Tosafot there daf 96 side 2 s.v. if, etc.:`,
    '4#ז': `Or even, etc. As is said there daf 108-109 — see there:`,
    '5#א': `In the baraita, etc. As is said in chapter 95 daf 96 side 2 I do not, etc., and Rav Huna said, etc.:`,
    '5#ב': `Lean kid. As is said there (daf 97 side 1) that lean one was, and as is said below:`,
    '5#ג': `If there is not, etc. Tosafot there s.v. that. And one might say, etc., and even the first answer agrees in this; and as is said there daf 108 side 1 a drop of milk, etc. — behold if there is not nosen taam it is all permitted — and not like Ra'ah:`,
    '5#ד': `Even though there is not in it sixty, etc. Rabbenu Tam wrote in Tosafot there daf 97 side 1: (collected) even though there is not in it sixty, etc. According to the first explanation in Tosafot there s.v. that, etc. — for on the other answer it is difficult, for the Gemara's flow implies Shmuel agrees in this; and for Shmuel he holds min b'mino b'mashahu; and there with those hides, etc., in its fat there was not sixty from its custom to forbid it — and nevertheless they permitted because it is like boiling that is roasted. Requires study (end of his words):`,
    '5#ה': `And even a piece, etc. Basar b'chalav and even Levi did not explain except because he holds reicha is not a matter:`,
    '5#ו': `And all this, etc. Tosafot there s.v. it is different and that which we say, etc. But Rashba wrote gravy spreads — nevertheless the place of its fall is forbidden; and there when there is no nosen taam it deals — but from Tosafot there it does not imply so; and so Rambam and Semak and Shulchan Aruch:`,
    '5#ז': `And every cooked, etc. As is said in Zevachim daf 97 side 1 one piece touched a piece and a thin strip, etc., and without gravy it deals — and a spreading thing forbids in all of it like in roasting from that of a drop of milk as above:`,
    '5#ח': `And even if there is, etc. Meaning even for those who disagree with Rashba — nevertheless we are concerned perhaps it does not spread d'rabbanan:`,
    '5#ט': `But in prohibition, etc. For expertise does not apply:`,
    '6#א': `A thing, etc. As above section 3:`,
    '6#ב': `And if it fell, etc. If, etc. As is said in the mishnah of a drop of milk.`,
    '7#א': `Or in basar b'chalav. This entire seif is Rashba's words — meaning meat touching cheese; but meat in which milk was absorbed is like any absorbed thing that does not forbid; and as Tosafot daf 108 side 1 s.v. a drop, etc., from that which we say in the Gemara there if you say do not shake at all, etc.; and so Magen Avraham chapter 9 law 8 explicitly in the name of Rashba — and these are his words: furthermore he wrote and according to his view do not shake at all — other pieces are not forbidden even though, etc., and all the more so for all other prohibitions that a piece forbidden because it received taste from elsewhere does not forbid its fellows by contact even if all are hot and even if they were roasted together and touched one another at roasting — absorbed does not go from piece to piece without gravy; and this is Rashba's view and he wrote that if it absorbed milk or forbidden fat that spreads — it too emits them hot in them without gravy; but in basar b'chalav it does not spread — meaning from piece to piece; and the later ones erred in this in several matters:`,
    '7#ב': `But if there is not, etc. As above from that of a drop of milk as Magen Avraham and Tosafot said; and so Tosafot daf 96 side 2 s.v. even — that we do not say the piece itself nosen taam — rather, etc., and therefore he said even if they were roasted, etc., as is said there but if roasted, etc., and Tosafot explained as much as a peel is forbidden and nevertheless it does not return and forbid:`,
    '7#ג': `In the baraita, etc. But, etc. So Rashba and Tur; but Tosafot daf 108 side 1 wrote a drop of milk even though it spreads does not go from piece to piece without gravy from what is said there if you say, etc., as above; and so in Mordechai simanim 727, 754, 759, 762; and so in hagahah in siman 92 section 2; and so Magen Avraham as above — except Rashba distinguishes specifically milk does not spread only in that piece; but milk or fat spreads even from piece to piece as Magen Avraham in his name as above; but Mordechai in the name of Maharam wrote the same for milk and proved from a drop of milk as above; and so in Shulchan Aruch section 9 milk does not, etc.; and so Mordechai in the name of Semak therefore Rashi there daf 95 side 1 s.v. in the basket a cabbage and did not explain a basket because in dry it does not spread from piece to piece from that of a drop of milk and he would not estimate in all of it; but Rashba explains basket as Tosafot there in the name of the Arukh from that of chapter 1 of Sanhedrin it is soft, etc., and so in chapter 2 of Bava Batra {Rama: (daf 22 side 1 and Rashi there s.v. basket-makers, etc., and one might say, etc., and our master copied Mordechai's words — see there)} those basket-makers — and therefore it must spread in all of them; but Rashi explained from the language basket as in chapter 2 of Pesachim and as Tosafot said there; and Tosafot there daf 100 side 1 s.v. in advance and R' Efraim would bring, etc., and even if we say, etc.:`,
    '7#ד': `Whether touching. According to his approach as is said in section 3 in hagahah:`,
    '7#ה': `Or, etc. Here he wrote specifically this on that as is said in the hagahah above:`,
    '7#ו': `But a vessel, etc. From what they argue daf 111 side 2 regarding fish that rose in a bowl:`,
    '8#א': `Kulya, etc. Tosafot there s.v. kulya, etc., and it appears, etc.: (collected) kulya, etc. Tosafot there s.v. kulya, etc., and you might ask, etc. (and one might say, etc.) and even though we do not hold like this version that is the wording of the matter there s.v. it is different and we do not hold thus as is said in section 9 and conclusion one might say the white of kulya does not spread according to the first version there in the fat of the gid and Tosafot explain according to the second version; and see above in hagahah and siman 64 section 20 in hagahah that all, etc., and so the white (end of his words):`,
    '8#ב': `And some forbid, etc. So in chapter Maharai seif 95 according to Semak who in Semak siman 204 daf 95 side 1 in hagahah wrote however it does not appear to permit even in a lean kid unless there is sixty according to the second version in Tosafot there s.v. that; and proof that milk spreads in the whole piece as is said in a drop of milk — all the more so milk even if lean; and furthermore besides the law that the lean one was and likewise the change of kulya in its milk was — that answer comes to uproot all the earlier ones; and in both of them one forbids in sixty; and such cases exist in the Talmud where three changes are answered — the last comes to uproot the earlier — end of his words; and furthermore lest the membrane diminishes as is said in chapter 1 of Chullin {Rama: (daf 9 side 1)} and behold the rabbi does not hold like Semak as is said in section 6 in hagahah; and some say we are not expert, etc., and specifically, etc., and also the reasoning of diminution is nothing as is said in Chullin there — only the upper we are concerned it diminishes; and what the rabbi wrote and so we practice — this is because we are not expert and we estimate all milk in sixty as above:`,
    '8#ג': `And if it was cooked, etc. According to his approach that in dabuk prohibition we say chadichah na'aseh nevelah:`,
    '8#ד': `And the same in the membrane, etc. There in Piskei Maharai and he learned from kulya as above — see there:`,
    '9#א': `Salted that is not, etc. In chapter How One Roasts (Pesachim daf 76 side 1) and chapter 105:`,
    '9#ב': `Its law, etc. There and there and chapter 95:`,
    '9#ג': `As much as a peel. There like boiling that is roasted; and Rashba's view that even in roasting a peel suffices as is said in chapter 25 touched its earthenware, etc., anointed with terumah oil, etc., and there hot into cold peels; and there and in chapter 25 but roasted requires a peel; and Rashba and Ran explain roasted boiling; and as is said in Shulchan Aruch in siman 91 section 7; and in chapter 1 of Chullin (daf 8 side 2) for one who says in the slaughterhouse boiling peels; and in chapter 25 (daf 107 side 2) although a peel is not required, etc.; and what is said in chapter 25 a drop fell from its gravy, etc. — gravy is different; but Tosafot are stringent with as much as removal of place; and see in chapter 95 daf 96 side 2 s.v. until, etc.; and Rashba wrote one should be stringent like their words; and nevertheless Rosh wrote there in salting one need not be stringent and a peel suffices since it is not actual roasting; and see below; but Rashba and Ran wrote its law is like roasting for everything as is said below; nevertheless they wrote in several places that a peel suffices — only regarding those hides Rosh wrote and with a peel or in nosen taam it suffices like boiling that is roasted — meaning like roasting for all as its law; and as is said regarding Shmuel but if roasted it peels and eats, etc. — with a peel or in nosen taam; nevertheless in several places they were general that a peel suffices; and behold even though in roasting he ruled like Tosafot here he inclined to be lenient in d'rabbanan and relied on what Rashba wrote in roasting one should be stringent like Tosafot in nosen taam in Torah prohibitions — it implies in d'rabbanan one may be lenient; and even though he did not rely on Rashba in roasting and did not distinguish and like Tosafot and Rosh he relied here also on Rosh who distinguishes between salting and roasting. And so a peel if it is, etc.; and in membranes one should be stringent — meaning because gid fat is d'rabbanan as is said in chapter 95 daf 92 side 2; and likewise tendons as is said there; but in membranes he is uncertain from what is said there and they are not liable — nevertheless perhaps forbidden from the Torah like half a measure {Rama: (see Pesachim chapter 3 side 2 Ravina said in the outer, etc., and Rashi there)} and Beit Yosef wrote as much as a peel in gid fat which is certainly d'rabbanan and in membranes where we doubt if they are d'oraisa perhaps nosen taam is also required. And see above siman 70 that he ruled a peel suffices even in d'oraisa as stated in section 3; and so in siman 91 section 5. And see in siman 64 section 20 that he ruled like Rambam that even a peel is not needed in tendons and membranes; and in the name of some he wrote as much as a peel. And see in siman 91 section 7 that he ruled that even in roasting only a peel is needed and per Rashba and Ran; and see there in hagahah that he wrote like Tosafot only that from his words it implies he agrees with Shulchan Aruch's ruling that wrote even if they are, etc.:`,
    '9#ד': `And if it is milk, etc. As is said in chapter 95 there in roasting milk is different, etc., and salted its law is like roasting for all as is said like boiling that is roasted; and Ravina there initially thought it is stricter than roasting — all the more so one should not reduce it from roasting; and there daf 98 in the incident of the ox that fell into the basket — Rashba and Ran explain basket per Tosafot in the name of the Arukh and they were salted there — behold we estimate in sixty; but in fat and tendons and membranes that are lean they do not spread as is said that lean one and as Tosafot said there s.v. it is different in the name of Rabbenu Tam: (collected) and if it is milk, etc. — the flow in chapter 28 daf 112-113 that they say forbidden and it implies entirely that a peel is forbidden as is said daf 97 side 2 father permits — and Tosefet Yom Tov and Maharshal explain it deals with a fatty thing (end of his words):`,
    '9#ה': `And if the meat, etc. Like in roasting in section 5 from that of chapter 25 and as stated above in siman 70 section 4 and in siman 91 section 6:`,
    '9#ו': `Even in fat, etc. And so even in a completely lean prohibition except he means even in d'rabbanan prohibition — not like Tosafot there in the name of R' Meir [and so Rashi daf 99 side 2 s.v. and the halachah, etc., and so Rif and Rosh and Shulchan Aruch] and as stated above siman 100 section 2; but its fat, etc., and as will be explained; and this that he did not mention here membranes and this reasoning of permitting imparts taste to prohibition and the absorption carries with it — he took from Tur's words in this siman regarding roasting and even a lean forbidden piece and a piece, etc., as is said in section 5. But it is not primary and all poskim disagree; and Tur himself in this siman wrote and that a forbidden piece forbids its fellow by contact — specifically when its prohibition is from itself, etc., but, etc., even the permitted piece whose fat and fat in it spread — because the fat in it does not carry the prohibition with it except to where the prohibition can go by its nature; and Beit Yosef in Shulchan Aruch section 7 omitted it because of that which is here; and the Acharonim strained themselves and it does not appear except that that disagrees; and so Tosafot daf 96 side 2 s.v. even. And even if there is fat in the meat, etc., that we do not say the piece, etc., and so Rosh there and as stated siman 69 section 18; and see in this siman section 14 in hagahah that wherever there is not, etc., and so Peri Chadash; and one might say for that of chapter 25 that reicha is different as is said in hagahah section 14 siman 35 from the difficulty above — see there:`,
    '9#ז': `And to take from the place, etc. As is said in section 5 in roasting and so, etc.:`,
    '9#ח': `In nosen taam and at least, etc. Because he is uncertain as above; and even though above he ruled as much as a peel there he also relied on Rambam who is lenient in them because they are lean and do not spread at all — unlike here where it spreads because of the fattiness:`,
    '9#ט': `And regarding other, etc. As Tosafot daf 108 side 1 s.v. a drop — and the same for milk; but Rashba holds milk spreads from piece to piece as is said in section 7 and the same for salting whose law is like roasting for all:`,
    '9#י': `And if there is not, etc. For a d'oraisa doubt is to be stringent:`,
    '9#כ': `And if it is known, etc. As is said in siman 109:`,
    '9#ל': `Even if they are, etc. As is said in siman 101 section 2:`,
    '9#מ': `And some say, etc. Meaning even in oil — and this is Ra'avyah and Ramban [and so Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit] and as Rosh said that in conclusion like boiling that is roasted is weaker than roasting; and that incident of the basket is explained per Rashi in a pot; and so it must be for Maharam's view that it does not spread from piece to piece as above:`,
    '9#נ': `And according to, etc. It refers to Shulchan Aruch's ruling that this is primary and he did not bring the Yesh Omrim except because he wants to conclude nevertheless one may rely, etc.:`,
    '9#ס': `Like in cooking that if, etc. Rashba's view in section 7 as above:`,
    '9#ע': `Opposite, etc. Chadichah na'aseh nevelah:`,
    '9#פ': `However, etc. Even for those who disagree with Rashba and hold that if there is sixty everything is permitted — nevertheless one should be concerned perhaps it is not fatty; and as above in section 5 in hagahah:`,
    '9#צ': `And if he does not know, etc. Since many disagree with Rashba and do not require a peel in oil in such a case one may rely on them; and Peri Chadash however in lean it was forbidden and acted, etc.:`,
    '9#ק': `And even though there is, etc. Meaning to the detriment of Maharam who requires sixty in each as above:`,
    '9#ר': `And all this in prohibition, etc. As stated above in section 5 in hagahah:`,
    '9#ש': `Such as chametz, etc. Even though it is b'mashahu as is said in Orach Chayyim siman 447 section 1 and the law of its mixture, etc., but, etc., and see there:`,
    '9#ת': `And in Orach Chayyim, etc. Siman 467 section 14 and there he wrote and some are stringent, etc. — see there:`,
    '10#א': `In the baraita when the prohibition, etc. In chapter 105 daf 113 side 1:`,
    '10#ב': `Rather rinsing. Chapter 25 {Rama: (daf 76 side 1)} and chapter 105 {Rama: (daf 107 side 2):}`,
    '10#ג': `And some forbid, etc. This is Ran's view and his reason: in a place of contact it becomes salted from the prohibition; and Rosh there wrote that in contact it is not like boiling as stated above until one salts it well well — and all the more so for Tosafot who specifically when salting on both sides from that of Menachot daf 21; and see in section 91 in hagahah as stated in the name of Chasdei David; and in siman 70 he wrote in Shulchan Aruch two explanations and here he ruled like the first explanation; and so in siman 91 section 8:`,
    '11#א': `This that we said, etc. Like in roasting in section 3 that it is like roasting for all; and so in siman 70 section 4 and as stated in section 3; and there is no distinction, etc. — this is with lean where for a peel there is no distinction; and so in siman 91 section 5:`,
    '11#ב': `And some disagree, etc. From the Gemara's distinction here between impure salted or pure brine and it does not distinguish between upper or lower; and in roasting the Gemara distinguishes between upper and lower and not between hot prohibition or permitted — the reason: granted with fire that which is hot overpowers by boiling its fellow or cooling it; but in salting the salting has no power to make the brine salted, for salted is not like boiling until one salts on both sides and salts well well; and likewise the brine has no power to make the salted brine — as is said and one rinses well well; therefore with fire even if the prohibition is cold the permitted is heated by them — unlike here; and even if the prohibition is above the lower has no power to make it brine. Semak siman 48:`,
    '12#א': `Cheeses, etc. Like pure salted and impure brine. Tosafot there s.v. pure, etc.:`,
    '12#ב': `And the same if, etc. For the reason of the permission is not that the vessel does not absorb in salting as Rosh wrote there and wood was not forbidden through salted like boiling, etc. — if so here it is forbidden; rather the reason is that what was absorbed does not emit — and as Tosafot said there salting does not apply to wood and wood does not become boiling through salting — meaning impure salted forbids because it became boiling — unlike wood that does not become boiling and is like brine that does not emit; therefore all the more so in other prohibition vessels that were never salted — in such a case even with two pieces it is permitted; and see in section 9 section 85 and section 86 and in hagahot there and there; and therefore he said and even, etc. For according to the first reason that it does not absorb when not perforated it would be forbidden because it absorbs as is said daf 111 side 2 a bowl in which one salted, etc., and Rosh's explanation there that it deals with a vessel not perforated — otherwise it would be forbidden to salt in it twice in one vessel; and according to his approach that salting emits from a vessel except that it does not absorb — and here forbidden to eat boiling therefore absorbs — end of his words; and not only for Ran and Shulchan Aruch who explain it deals even in a perforated vessel as is said in siman 69 section 19 — rather even for Rosh who wrote the reason because blood drips slickly — unlike brine and other prohibition; and proof from Rav Mari's incident there and Rashi there daf 113 side 1 s.v. the pure, etc., and s.v. but its brine, etc., and Tosafot s.v. and let us say, etc.; and see above siman 91 section 66 and brine of, etc. And for this reason Ra'avyah disagreed with Tosafot and forbids even in a perforated vessel as Mordechai and Hagahot said there s.v. and Ra'avyah disagrees, etc. — see there — for from the Gemara there bowl salted, etc., proves it absorbs from salting except that it does not emit as Tosafot said as above; and therefore he said and the same, etc., that it does not, etc., to emit, etc., and he said there is no salted like boiling, etc. — meaning even though we say a bowl in which one salted is forbidden to eat boiling in it — behold it emits to this — therefore he said there is no salted, etc., that specifically in actual boiling it emits even without gravy — and not as Mordechai and Hagahot in siman 49 in Rosh s.v. wrote Ra'avyah in the bowl, etc.; and it appears that what Ra'avyah explained regarding fat, etc. And it is not specifically with two pieces we say thus and not in a vessel as is said in section 7 in hagahah — rather as above; and therefore Tosafot and wood does not become boiling — for if it became boiling it would be forbidden without gravy:`,
    '12#ג': `But, etc. Concern for Ra'avyah's words; and also Rosh wrote b'dieved; and even Tosafot wrote and one who is stringent, etc., as is said in section 13:`,
    '13#א': `Salt, etc. For the reason above. Tosafot there:`,
    '13#ב': `And the same, etc. For the reason of the permission is not because it is nosen taam bar nosen taam — therefore here where nosen taam bar nosen taam does not apply because it is prohibition {Rama: (Zevachim daf 96 side 2 and conclusion)} a pot in which meat was cooked, etc., in nosen taam — because the second taste in prohibition; and as Tosafot in Zevachim daf 96 side 1 s.v. and if, etc. — and it is not so that because of its sharpness it is considered one taste as Tosafot said there and in Chullin daf 111 side 2 s.v. the halachah; and as is said in siman 96 section 3 — rather as above that it does not emit since there is no strain as Rashi daf 112 side 1 s.v. gourd. And furthermore because, etc., and because, etc.:`,
    '13#ג': `Since they are, etc. Meaning therefore permitted even l'chatchila to put in a prohibition vessel as Tosafot said in Avodah Zarah daf 32 side 1 s.v. and behold:`,
    '13#ד': `They do not absorb, etc. See above siman 91 section 5; and if they are dry, etc. — only they need rinsing because the prohibition touched in substance — unlike here where the prohibition is not in substance; and therefore he said only, etc.:`,
    '13#ה': `And one who is stringent, etc. Tosafot there:`,
    '14#א': `Absorbed salt, etc. Tosafot there daf 108 side 2 s.v. why, etc., and from here, etc.:`,
    '14#ב': `Even though, etc. Meaning even though it is not nullified in sixty as is said regarding spices made for taste, etc., in Chullin and daf 6 side 1 daf 97 side 2 and in Avodah Zarah daf 69 side 1:`,
    '14#ג': `Nosen taam in a pot. For he does not hold in a thousand — rather all this is nosen taam as Tosafot said there side 1 s.v. spices and daf 69 side 1 s.v. in spices, etc.:`,
    '14#ד': `For wherever, etc. Tosafot chapter 95 daf 96 side 2 s.v. even:`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#ב': `["And if it is kavush inside brine or vinegar, etc. — the same as cooked. The Mechaber's view is like Rosh to distinguish between brine and other things: in brine we do not require me'eit l'eit because the boiling of the salt suffices when it rests the measure that one would put it on the fire and it begins to boil; and he equated vinegar to brine; and he follows Beit Yosef who wrote: kavush in brine — Rosh wrote at end of chapter 25 that its measure is as if placed on the fire until it boils and begins to cook; if kavush inside brine in this measure, everything inside the brine is forbidden; in less than this measure only as much as a peel, the law of salting — behold it is like boiling — end of his words. One might wonder: behold Rosh himself wrote in his commentary at end of Avodah Zarah that even in vinegar we require a full day; and Beit Yosef brought it in this siman — but certainly one should distinguish vinegar from brine: brine that we forbid because of the power of salt in it suffices in the measure that one puts it on the fire and it boils, because salted is like boiling as is explained in Rosh — unlike vinegar; and the Mechaber himself wrote in siman 104 therefore if it fell into beer or vinegar in cold and he removed it whole — if it did not rest me'eit l'eit it is permitted — requires study. (Again I saw in Sefer Shareit Yehudah he brought Beit Yosef's responsum where he apologized for his words in Beit Yosef that he agrees in vinegar one needs me'eit l'eit and his words in Shulchan Aruch here are strained to reconcile — see there.) Also Bach ruled that in vinegar it is kavush immediately like brine; and he wrote Rosh agrees to this — and it is not so as I explained; also in Derishah he directed Rosh's view to another view that is incorrect because he wanted to reconcile what Hagahot Maimoniyot wrote in the name of Rosh (I did not find this in the new Hagahot before me) that when we say kavush kemevushal — this is in vinegar and brine as Rashi explained — end of his words; and this needs no answer, for certainly Hagahot Maimoniyot did not mean this Rosh but another such as Rosh of Palermo or Rosh of Lunel, as is Hagahot Maimoniyot and Mordechai's way to bring them; rather it appears like Rosh and Tur and Beit Yosef that vinegar equals other liquids. And so Mordechai end of chapter 2 of Beitzah; and so Maharshal in Orach Shemesh and in his book chapter 95 seif 49 and chapter 105 siman 91; and so Orach Chayyim general rule 30 law 8 that in vinegar it is not kavush except me'eit l'eit — see there. And so in Sefer Alef-Resh daf 37 side 2 and daf 54 side 2: Orach Chayyim wrote general rule 30 section 9: thin milk that rested me'eit l'eit in a rinsed meat bowl of the same day — they are forbidden even b'dieved; and the same for other permitted steeped in a prohibition vessel of the same day — end of his words; and he copied in Sefer Alef-Resh daf 54 side 2. One might say: since it only became kavush after me'eit l'eit — if so, after me'eit l'eit it is nosen taam lifgam; and also in general rule 33 end section 3 he wrote if it rested even lukewarm where yad soledes bo does not apply — in this it never becomes same-day at all, for the cold food in it does not make the taste of absorption in the vessel walls for the better, even though regarding prohibition we forbid in such a case if it rested me'eit l'eit in a same-day prohibition vessel as is said above in the responsum kavush — nevertheless the taste is impaired — end of his words; and his words are not understood, for if it rested me'eit l'eit the taste is also impaired. I found in the rabbi's handwritten gloss on Orach Chayyim general rule 34 section 15 that he sensed this difficulty and wrote it deals when the prohibition is somewhat in substance — the language of same-day does not imply this; and furthermore in general rule 30 he wrote rinsed bowl — requires study. Again I found in responsum of R' Leib ibn Chaviv siman 138 beginning daf 242 in responsum on lupines in gentile cooking — it implies there explicitly that it is permitted; and in any case there are poskim that kavush is only in three days; and in any case many poskim that overnight impairs; and as above section 103:"]`,
    '1#א': `["And some are lenient regarding what is outside the brine. From the rabbi's words in Turei Zahav general rule 22 section 4 it appears this is primary. And so Rosh and Rashba in chapter 105 regarding meat salted in an unperforated vessel; and even a permitted piece inside the brine that is fatty; and so Taz siman 69 section 18 and other poskim. And we forbid there meat salted in an unperforated vessel even what is outside the brine — but this was explained there s.k. 88: the reason is because when salted in an unperforated vessel the blood spread from place to place — see there. And perhaps for this the rabbi hinted in Bedek Malkhut; but certainly if the prohibition is fatty, even what is outside the brine is forbidden, as the poskim imply there; and so Tur at the end of this section; and Orach Chayyim general rule 30 section 10; and general rule 10. Therefore it would appear to forbid for us what is outside the pickling, since we do not distinguish between lean and fatty as below in section 5 and section 9 in hagahah — unless one might say the rabbi should not write a leniency here since for us all forbid; and all the more he should not rely on Turei Zahav's lenient reasoning as primary; and furthermore in section 70 he wrote that meat which fell into brine — what is outside is permitted; and Orach Chayyim also holds we do not distinguish lean from fatty, yet in pickling he permits what is outside; therefore it appears that even though we do not distinguish, this applies only in salting and roasting and similar cases, but we are not so stringent in all respects; and so I wrote in s.k. 17 — see there:"]`,
    '1#ג': `["And in less than this measure, etc. The rabbi also agrees to this that it is not pickling in less than this measure, as Bedek Malkhut also wrote; however at any rate salted like boiling applies immediately to forbid the whole thing, as is said in Turei Zahav general rule 6 section 9 and in section 70 in hagahah; and nosen taam — where forbidden fell into permitted brine the prohibition is d'rabbanan and the permitted items are completely lean: in salting even for the rabbi it forbids only as much as a peel, as is said in this siman s.k. 38; and in pickling, in the measure that one would put it on the fire and it boils, it forbids the whole thing — investigate:"]`,
    '1#ד': `["And see above, etc. And see in siman 70 in s.k. 36 and in siman 69 section 18 and what is written there:"]`,
    '2#_': `One should be concerned to be cautious l'chatchila in this matter. Meaning even if it is something customarily rinsed afterward — when it is permitted to place it on cold prohibition as above siman 91 — nevertheless here one should be cautious l'chatchila in this matter; and in my books I expanded on the laws of pouring and kli sheni; and here I write in utmost brevity what relates to the roots of the law: Rashbam whom Tosafot brought in chapter Blood of Sin Offering (Zevachim daf 95 side 1) and chapter Kirah (Shabbos daf 42 side 2) and Rosh and Ran — pouring is like kli sheni and does not cook; and likewise does not emit and absorb at once like kli sheni. Rabbenu Tam whom all those poskim brought — pouring cooks as much as a peel like kli rishon; and it is proved that Rashbam nevertheless pouring absorbs as much as a peel — e.g. if one poured forbidden boiling on permitted or milk on meat it forbids as much as a peel — only it does not emit and absorb at once; therefore permitted to pour on the chicken to pluck; and also proved that Rashbam holds kli sheni does not absorb at all even as much as a peel; and Rabbenu Tam whom all those poskim brought — it is proved he holds that just as pouring does not cook in all of it, so it does not absorb in all of it — unlike Maharshal in responsum 62 who wrote that for Rabbenu Tam whom Rosh brought it absorbs in all of it; and in Perishah section 68 he wrote that for Rabbenu Tam whom Rosh brought it cooks in all of it — this is astounding; and also proved from above that Rabbenu Tam holds kli sheni does not absorb as much as a peel; and if so the view of all those poskim above and Semag from them stands in Rabbenu Tam's approach — unlike what Maharshal wrote in Orach Shemesh section 33 that for Semag even earthenware absorbs in kli sheni — all the more so food; and so many Rishonim and Acharonim that kli sheni does not absorb at all; and so the rabbi in Turei Zahav general rule 33 law 1. One might say in hagahah in Orach Chayyim siman 451 it implies he holds earthenware used for chametz in kli sheni is forbidden — perhaps because of chametz stringency it touched it; nevertheless one should be stringent and forbid in earthenware where there is not so much loss, since Ramban and Rashba in Torat HaBayit daf 92 hold even copper absorbs; and there are poskim that even though kli sheni does not cook, nevertheless it absorbs and emits; and in food one may peel where there is not much loss; but in any case one does not forbid the whole thing in kli sheni; and so Maharshal in responsum there did not write it absorbs except as much as a peel. One might question what he wrote in his book chapter 105 siman 71 to forbid the whole thing. And in Bach in this siman he is very stringent that kli sheni emits and absorbs at once and forbids the whole thing — this is astounding; and we hold leniently for pouring that kli rishon cooks as much as a peel; therefore for Shabbos and basar b'chalav it is forbidden; and even in pouring where the stream broke off it nevertheless absorbs as much as a peel for all — as the poskim said and as is proved in chapter 25 (daf 76 side 1); and even though from the rabbi's words siman 68 section 10 it appears that if one poured from kli rishon on the chicken to pluck and the stream broke — one need not even a peel, because it does not emit and absorb at once — nevertheless it absorbs; and this is implied from Tosafot in Zevachim and Mordechai and Hagahot and Ran at end of Avodah Zarah; nevertheless certainly a peel suffices and it does not absorb more — and this is proved from what the poskim and Taz siman 91 section 4 wrote: boiling milk fell on cold meat — peel the meat and it is permitted; and so in the Talmud chapter 25 there. And all heat means specifically where yad soledes bo applies; but where yad soledes bo does not apply, even kli rishon standing on the fire does not forbid at all; and so early and late poskim in many places; and so Maharshal chapter 105 siman 71 — except afterward he wrote he is concerned to be lenient in kli rishon on the fire as long as it is hot even if yad soledes bo does not apply, because they made a distance for kli rishon as in Yerushalmi — I did not reach the end of his view, for in Yerushalmi chapter Kirah it says they made a distance for kli rishon where the hand does not rule — meaning yad soledes bo does not apply; but where the hand rules it is proved there explicitly in Yerushalmi that all permit; and so the consensus of poskim; and so in Turei Zahav general rule 57 law 12 — except in general rule 33 section 3 he ruled like Rashba and Tur that cold into hot where yad soledes bo does not apply requires a peel; and one must say he holds that even though yad soledes bo does not apply it absorbs as much as a peel; and one may be stringent even though from other poskim it does not appear so — requires study.`,
    '3#א': `["Everything is forbidden. Meaning in a gravy item or in fatty roasting and similar — unlike lean according to Taz; and for us in every case it is forbidden:"]`,
    '3#ב': `["But if already placed in kli sheni, etc. Specifically if already in kli sheni; but if one placed forbidden in a permitted bowl — the bowl needs a peel; and if one placed permitted in a forbidden bowl — the permitted needs a peel, for it is like pouring — so Bedek Malkhut; and so it appears from his words here in hagahah; and see in s.k. 9:"]`,
    '3#ג': `["If both are hot from the heat of kli rishon, etc. Maharshal's view in Orach Shemesh and in his book chapter 105 siman 71: in a solid item such as a piece of meat or fish or rice or millet that one pours hot from the pot — if yad soledes bo applies its law is always like kli rishon; and I cannot decide — rather it appears since Tosafot chapter Kirah wrote the reason to distinguish kli rishon from kli sheni — because kli rishon stood on the fire its walls are hot and hold heat a long time — therefore everything depends on the walls; therefore a solid item too, since it is called dry, walls do not apply; and so Orach Chayyim general rule 37 section 7 that in a dry item there are no cooling walls and it cooks forever as long as yad soledes bo applies — see there; and so it appears initially in end of Shulchan David on flies and in Orach Chayyim general rule 57 section 3 in Mordechai's name: if one poured food with a fly into a bowl and the fly fell first — the bowl is forbidden as much as a peel like hot into cold, for a fly coming from boiling cannot be like kli sheni — end of his words; and as Maharshal explained there — though I found the rabbi's handwritten gloss on Orach Chayyim general rule 27: it appears to me he speaks from the reason of pouring — since the fly mixed with the food it is still called kli rishon like other pouring that forbids as much as a peel; but without this there is no distinction between fly and other pouring — end of his words; the rabbi has his own reason here in hagahah and above siman 94 section 4 follows his reason; and so Turei Zahav end of general rule 61 that the custom is when eating meat in a bowl with a milk knife we do not forbid — but the plain sense of Shulchan David and those poskim implies like Maharshal; and so it appears initially from Beit Yosef siman 107 and as Perishah wrote there explicitly — see there (and see siman 107 and investigate); and the rabbi in Bedek Malkhut wrote in siman 107 we also come from pouring — and he rejected Maharshal's words without compelling proofs — requires study; nevertheless certainly in rice and millet if clear and poured like gravy it appears one may rely on the rabbi's words; and so Orach Chayyim implies there that specifically in dry items kli sheni does not apply; and perhaps Maharshal meant only rice and millet that are not clear — and so his words there imply — see there; and Bach also wrote opinions from his heart that are not compelling — the conclusion I wrote:"]`,
    '3#ד': `["And if the one is cold the permitted needs a peel. Meat and cheese that touched each other and one of them was cold — both need a peel; and so Orach Chayyim implies there; and it is simple; and Maharshal wrote in Orach Shemesh and in his book chapter 25 section 70: if the prohibition is hot it appears simple per Semak's view to distinguish between heat of fire and salting that all is forbidden; and I wonder, for Semak (siman 200 daf 96 beginning side 2) wrote at end of his words in chapter Blood of Sin Offering we say piece with piece, thin strip with thin strip — peel its place; one might say what is different from hot with hot that is forbidden, etc. — and perhaps one might say there with hot it deals this on that and there with thin strip it deals side by side — end of his words; and Mordechai brought it in chapter 105 — behold even when both are hot they permit with a peel; and so Orach Chayyim brought there; and Maharshal himself in Orach Shemesh siman 28 on his name — see there:"]`,
    '3#ה': `["Forbidden prohibition that he placed in a permitted vessel, etc. This is Orach Chayyim's view; and so Rashba in his novellae (chapter 105 and Torat HaBayit daf 73): according to those who say a bowl in which one salted other meat after rinsing is cold — if one put hot meat in it a peel suffices whether in dry boiling or wet boiling — hot into cold, tattei overpowers; and with a peel it suffices; and so our teachers Tosafot — end of his words; and Maharshal chapter 95 section 45 and chapter 105 section 60 divided Avodah Zarah and wrote: it appears to me this reasoning has no basis at all, since I proved from all the great ones that in a same-day bowl it forbids a hot item without gravy because it has no emission from itself (as the rabbi section 7 said) — therefore one cannot say regarding bowl or spoon upper overpowers or lower overpowers except with two food types whose nature is hot and cold — unlike a vessel whose nature is not so; therefore if next to it it emits and absorbs — therefore whenever it is hot where yad soledes bo applies it forbids; and it is also forbidden from the prohibition itself in the vessel where there is no sixty, for we hold heat of roasting and heat of cooking are all in sixty — end of his words. In the Talmud chapter 25 regarding a drop fell from its gravy it does not initially imply so; and so it is proved explicitly in Tosafot chapter 25 (daf 111 side 2 beginning s.v. the halachah) and Rosh there regarding fish that rose in a meat bowl and one of them was hot — whether for one who says upper overpowers or lower overpowers, a peel is required — end of his words; it implies even in a vessel we say tattei overpowers; and so Rif in Hagahot Teshuvot siman 249; and so Shulchan David at end of the book on flies; and so Beit Yosef sections 94 and 95 and the rabbi and Acharonim; and Rashba in his novellae chapter 105 daf 117 side 3 and Orach Chayyim general rule 36 and the rabbi (and as below section 7) also hold that a vessel forbids a hot item without gravy — or we do not depend on this at all; for although it forbids hot without gravy — this is where both are hot or the lower is hot to forbid as much as removal; and for us the upper hot forbids as much as a peel — unlike if it did not forbid without gravy it would need neither removal nor peel; and Maharshal connected this to that — I do not know what one has to do with the other; I also wonder at him, for he himself ruled in chapter 105 that if one put boiling gravy in a bowl in which meat was salted, sixty against the peel suffices; and if you say tattei does not apply in a vessel we need sixty against the whole thing, for he holds there in siman 71 the whole vessel is forbidden in salting — requires study:"]`,
    '5#ב': `["And every thing that has spreading, etc. Requires removal of place; and Maharai's view is wherever it is nullified in sixty one does not need removal or peel; and Maharshal in Orach Shemesh and chapter 95 siman 28 wrote: in lean one should require peel in salting and removal in roasting even if there is sixty; but in fatty substance Maharai and one who is stringent walks like a fool in darkness — end of his words; and so Maharam of Padua in responsum to the rabbi siman 90 — see there; and the rabbi's view in Turei Zahav beginning of general rule 37 regarding roasting and beginning of general rule 38 regarding salting like the Mechaber; and so here in hagahah; and so the famous early poskim hold:"]`,
    '7#א': `["That which says a forbidden piece, etc., or basar b'chalav, etc. It implies this is a meat piece in which milk was absorbed and the whole piece is considered the body of the prohibition — as the poskim said in siman 92 (whom I brought there s.k. 10); and Taz wrote above siman 101: it has no status of a piece fit to be honored unless its prohibition is from itself such as nevelah and basar b'chalav, etc.; and he also explained this is meat in which milk was absorbed; and so Maharai (in Hagahot Shulchan David siman 91) and Bedek Malkhut and Derishah section 23 and other Acharonim understood Tur's words which are like the Mechaber's — that a meat piece with absorbed milk forbids its fellow by contact without gravy. But Rashba wrote in Torat HaBayit (house 4 gate 1) on these matters that even in basar b'chalav the milk does not spread — and even though they made it like a nevelah piece they did not make it like nevelah itself for this matter — end of his words. In Maharai's writings siman 249 he wrote in his teacher's name: I am also uncertain about meat that received taste from milk when there was not sixty to nullify it and it became forbidden and again touched while hot other meat — Mordechai (chapter 105) in R' Yehudah's name and Semak that it does not forbid without gravy from the sugya of a drop of milk; and they proved this reasoning from that which was shaken and covered; and it is difficult, for Semak wrote from that which is written in Semak that basar b'chalav is different from other prohibitions — behold chalav is not nullified — specifically when it is the root of the prohibition and not when forbidden through absorption of prohibition; and regarding meat forbidden from milk we do not say thus, and it is not nullified; and even though it is a complete mishnah in Avodah Zarah that chalav of basar b'chalav is not nullified — until here his words. Also in his gates he wondered at Semak and Mordechai in R' Yehudah's name and supported them on this; and he wrote: it appears to answer somewhat — certainly in basar b'chalav as long as it is mixed it is called the root and body of the prohibition; and even the meat without milk is like kilayim in cloth: when flax thread is mixed in a wool garment it is forbidden to cover with the whole garment even on the side where there is no flax thread; and if cut from the garment that side without flax — that side is permitted; so too it is established for the rabbis that no taste goes from this piece except meat taste alone and no milk unless through gravy — and it is as if Elijah came and told us a small piece was cut from this meat with no milk taste and it is permitted, like kilayim — requires study — end of his words; and so Maharshal in Orach Shemesh and in his book chapter 95 siman 45; and so it is compelled initially from the rabbi siman 92 who wrote if a drop of milk fell on a piece in the pot and he did not shake or cover at all — only that piece is forbidden and the rest of the pot is permitted; and if he held that in basar b'chalav once it is itself nevelah and considered body of prohibition it forbids its fellows without gravy — then what is in the pot would be forbidden for us who hold hot with hot without gravy forbids all (as Raavad challenged in Ma'akhalot Assurot chapter 9 and Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit daf 100); and if so necessarily it must be explained here that hot meat touched hot cheese — as Maharshal explained in Orach Shemesh and his book and Bach brings; and this is not primary — rather it appears the rabbi holds that although we hold hot with hot without gravy forbids all — this is because we are not expert between lean and fatty; nevertheless what is not body of prohibition only through absorption is not so stringent; and we establish it on the law that forbidden absorption does not spread without gravy; and milk does not spread — as Rashba said and as in siman 92 s.k. 3; and so I wrote there in Ran's name; also I proved there that this is the poskim's and Taz's view that lean milk prohibition is called and does not spread — see there; and proof: Turei Zahav general rule 28 wrote generally that if one absorbed non-spreading prohibition it does not forbid by contact even though we are not expert between lean and fatty — only we are certainly not so stringent; also Maharshal himself wrote in Orach Shemesh there that he strains to explain Tur's words thus; and Bach in siman 92 also understood Tur like Maharai — except he challenged there from that which the Talmud says in chapter 105 absorbs absorbs emits does not emit — and he answered it is only so the other pieces are not entirely forbidden but removal is certainly required — end of his words; and it may be so, for the Talmud did not say thus there but before we established chadichah na'aseh nevelah as is explained there explicitly; but after the conclusion one may squeeze it — forbidden; and chadichah na'aseh nevelah — therefore the meat is considered body of prohibition and forbids; and so Ran end of chapter 25 and the rabbi's Maggid in Ma'akhalot Assurot chapter 9 in Raavad's name and Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit there; and therefore what Maharshal wrote in his book and what Maharai and the stringent wrote in all this — investigate — I see no place for this stringency, etc., that absorbed prohibition does not spread without gravy even in basar b'chalav, etc., as R' Yehudah proved clearly from the sugya of a drop of milk, etc.; and Tosefet Yom Tov was not precise, etc. — end of his words — he was not precise, for there is no clear proof here, as is said; and it may be that R' Yehudah in Mordechai and Semak and other poskim also hold thus and only brought proof for other prohibitions from that sugya of a drop of milk that says in the Talmud absorbs absorbs emits does not emit — before it was thought chadichah na'aseh nevelah and body of prohibition; and even though Maharai in his writings and Hagahot Shulchan David did not understand their words thus — nevertheless it is not compelled — see there; and proof for my words in Semag daf 91 beginning side 3 he also wrote R' Yehudah's words and Mahara Shtain explained his words that absorbed basar b'chalav forbids like nevelah — see there; and in Ba'er Sheva he challenged Maharash on this that it is not correct — investigate; and so Orach Chayyim end of general rule 29 section 6 considers basar b'chalav prohibition from itself even for this; nevertheless it appears one should not forbid more than as much as removal, since otherwise we hold hot prohibition does not forbid hot permitted without gravy more than removal — only we are stringent because we are not expert between lean and fatty; and in such a case one should not be stringent — as is said; and so Orach Chayyim general rule 28 section 4: a meat piece that absorbed milk and touched another piece needs a peel — and peel does not mean specifically but removal. Again I found in Orach Shemesh Maharshal printed in Lublin daf 33 a hagahah from Maharshal who brought conversely proof from chapter 105 that regarding basar b'chalav a piece forbids its fellow without gravy; and he wrote there so ruled his teacher the Gaon — see there:"]`,
    '7#ב': `["Even if they were roasted together. And so the rabbi ruled in Turei Zahav general rule 28 section 1; and further he ruled there in Orach Chayyim's name: that which says a piece forbids its fellow by contact — specifically when its prohibition is from itself; but a piece forbidden from prohibition through absorption does not forbid by contact even if roasted or salted together — and this is when it absorbed non-spreading prohibition; but if it absorbed spreading prohibition it forbids by contact — end of his words; and I already wrote in the preceding s.k. that the rabbi's and Orach Chayyim's view is that although we do not distinguish lean from fatty — this is in the body of the matter; but what became forbidden through absorption we are not so stringent; and it is proved there in Turei Zahav that blood is not considered spreading prohibition for this; and so in many Talmud places and poskim that blood does not spread; and although we hold everywhere also for blood to estimate in sixty because we do not distinguish lean from fatty as the rabbi wrote in section 9 — nevertheless where the piece absorbed blood it does not forbid other pieces through salting and roasting; nevertheless it appears this is specifically when it was salted and rinsed as required and afterward absorbed blood — as in Orach Chayyim end of general rule 12 in such a case it does not go from piece to piece through salting and roasting; but meat not yet salted that still has its own blood — it is simple in the Talmud and all poskim that they forbid in salting and roasting — as explained in section 70; and Maharshal in Orach Shemesh and his book chapter 95 siman 45 wrote specifically with two dry hot items through cooking we hold thus — but in roasting and salting we properly say it spreads from piece to piece — for us even in non-fatty prohibition; and pay no attention to Rashba and Tur who wrote even in roasting it does not forbid without gravy because he holds even in roasting it does not spread — end of his words; and Orach Chayyim's view is like the rabbi's — and this is primary:"]`,
    '7#ג': `["If roasted together. Even the piece in which prohibition was absorbed — fat in it spreads because the fat in it does not carry the prohibition with it except to where the prohibition itself can go by nature — end of Tur; and this is the consensus of poskim and the Mechaber who omitted it — because Beit Yosef challenged these words and wrote: one should wonder at these words that contradict what he said above in section 5: even a lean forbidden piece and a permitted piece roasted with it — forbidden fat spreads in all of it — end of his words. And this is like what is explained there: the permitted goes and imparts taste to the prohibition and the prohibition returns and imparts to the permitted; and if so the fat carries the prohibition with it; and although the law here is agreed by all poskim as Beit Yosef said and the law above was not mentioned by any posek — nevertheless the Mechaber was concerned to be stringent above in section 5 and here he was general and did not say forbidden or permitted — this appears the Mechaber's intent; but this is not primary — rather it appears certainly this law here is true, agreed by all poskim; and that above is not difficult as Maharshal and Perishah and Bach explained: where prohibition is forbidden from itself — there he said above that even lean forbidden, fatty permitted carries prohibition because it must emit forbidden gravy and forbids as much as removal; therefore if permitted is fatty it holds its emission and absorbs in all of it — unlike here where the piece is not forbidden from itself but absorbed prohibition; and the rabbi in Bedek Malkhut answered: above it deals with lean milk — therefore since milk spreads though lean does not spread, fatty permitted imparts taste to it — end of his words; and see in s.k. 13:"]`,
    '7#ד': `["It forbids its fellow, etc. It appears here even for Taz it forbids all of it up to sixty against the absorbed prohibition — for the reason depends on the absorbed prohibition itself spreading and emerging; and so Orach Chayyim general rule 29 section 4 explicitly:"]`,
    '7#ה': `["Or the lower hot and the upper cold. So Rashba in Torat HaBayit; and it implies: if the lower is cold — even a peel is not needed; and the reason appears: for we hold hot into cold peels because before it cools it cannot but absorb somewhat — and this is specifically from the body of the item next to it it absorbs immediately before cooling since it touches; but it does not go from piece to piece before cooling:"]`,
    '7#ו': `["But a vessel that absorbed prohibition, etc. Since it has no emission from itself it forbids all the permitted; and it appears specifically food of permitted; but a vessel does not forbid a vessel without gravy — as the rabbi said sections 92 and 93 regarding two pots; and so Maharshal in Orach Shemesh and his book chapter 95 siman 45; and he further wrote there: but a dish such as rice or fish in which meat or milk was absorbed — if one sticks a spoon of meat or milk of the same day it properly forbids the absorption and the dish is considered as if all meat or milk and is forbidden with the spoon — end of his words:"]`,
    '7#ז': `["It forbids permitted, etc. And he wrote on this in Bedek Malkhut: and it is simple it forbids only as much as a peel without gravy as explained above — end of his words; and even though with two dry food items we hold it forbids all in a vessel as is different — as in siman 94 s.k. 32 that the rabbi's view is so — and although from Maharshal's words I brought in s.k. 9 it implies he holds even in a vessel it forbids entirely — one might say it deals with somewhat moist dry where therefore since by law it emits, it forbids as much as removal — as in siman 69 s.k. 60 for us it forbids up to sixty — unlike completely dry that only emits a peel; however in oily prohibition absorbed in a vessel perhaps even in completely dry it forbids in sixty; and although we do not distinguish lean from fatty — this is in the body of the matter; but what became forbidden through absorption we are not stringent — as in several places in this siman; nevertheless requires study for practical law:"]`,
    '9#ב': `["And to require removal of place for them. But sixty is not required; and so in Turei Zahav end of general rule 37; and although we hold even in brine and even in completely lean to estimate in sixty as will be explained — nevertheless gid fat is only d'rabbanan as explained in siman 65 section 8; and so in Turei Zahav end of general rule 38 — however this is for the main law; but to be concerned for custom the rabbi wrote siman 64 section 20 that where there is not slight loss one should require sixty — see there:"]`,
    '9#ג': `["And if the salted meat is fatty, etc. And even in gid fat, etc. — requires study, for it should be a greater novelty even in completely lean as above section 5 in roasting; and so in Beit Yosef; and on the essence of the law Maharshal challenged in his hagahot on Tur and Bach brings: even if we say thus — this applies only for roasting where fire heat imparts from one to the other and both are hot since they roasted together; but in salting we found no such reasoning; and also in roasting itself it is a novelty and no author distinguished — rather we rule generally wherever prohibition cannot go and spread, even if the permitted piece is fatty it is permitted — end of his words; and so Rashba in Torat HaBayit implies and Beit Yosef brings that specifically fatty prohibition is required — see there; nevertheless it appears even Taz did not say this except when both are salted; but not when prohibition was salted and permitted was brine — since brine has no power to impart taste to prohibition:"]`,
    '9#ה': `["And regarding other pieces, etc. to end of seif. In truth this is Maharam's view whom Mordechai brought at end of chapter 95; but I did not understand what he said in the beginning and what in the end — for in the beginning when it touched all of them and when unknown if it touched all — therefore also when unknown only one of them is forbidden — per the parable: ten pieces each with fifty and nine against the milk — if you say one was forbidden because milk was absorbed in it without sixty to nullify it — then the rest are not forbidden again; and if so necessarily only one is forbidden and nullified by majority; and so R' Nattaniel whom Mordechai brought — however from Maharam himself one might learn the reason when it touched one of them we do not know how much emerged and the pieces became forbidden and the milk returned and absorbed from the permitted piece and the permitted also became prohibition — for he holds in all prohibitions chadichah na'aseh nevelah; and so when it touched all of them — as Orach Chayyim general rule 54 section 2 wrote regarding milk that fell into two pots one after the other — each needs sixty to nullify all the milk (therefore Maharam and R' Nattaniel do not disagree — although from Maharam's words it implies if certainly touched all, each needs sixty to nullify milk as Beit Yosef — nevertheless it appears it deals when the pieces are salted so pieces also emit and absorb in milk; and R' Nattaniel deals when pieces are not salted — as we hold everywhere only the salted emits, not the brine — unlike Beit Yosef who said they disagree). But the Mechaber who does not hold chadichah na'aseh nevelah in other prohibitions — difficult; requires study. And Orach Chayyim general rule 14 section 8 wrote on these laws: we do not hold at all that whatever enters in doubt assists to nullify — end of his words; and he does not mean we do not hold this at all, for he himself at end of general rule 26 copied Rashba and Tur generally that whatever enters in doubt assists to nullify; and the Mechaber below siman 111 — rather he means here since it is min b'eno mino we do not hold thus — as in siman 92 s.k. 8 Rashba and Tur agree — see there:"]`,
    '9#כ': `["And although there is some leniency in this if the prohibition was fatty and spreads. Meaning when prohibition is not dabuk in the piece — all combine to nullify the milk even if not touching all — like in cooking; and so in Turei Zahav general rule 38 section 3; and Taz explained: and although there is some leniency — meaning that through measure of sixty we also permit the peel, etc.; one might say: behold the rabbi wrote and nevertheless that piece which the prohibition touched needs a peel; and also in section 5 and section 9 they ruled even if there is sixty a peel is needed at least; and so in Turei Zahav as is said there; but the matter is simple as I explained; and further proved in Turei Zahav that even if pieces do not touch milk but touch the piece that touched milk — all combine if prohibition is not dabuk like cooking — see there — requires study:"]`,
    '9#מ': `["For all, salting forbids only as much as a peel. Nevertheless the custom is not to distinguish) — however in d'rabbanan one may be lenient — end of his words in Turei Zahav end of general rule 38; and it implies even where there is not slight loss; but in prohibition where fattiness applies, even though only d'rabbanan like gid fat and tendons — one should be stringent where there is not slight loss as in s.k. 27 — because in any case they are like completely lean d'rabbanan:"]`,
    '11#ב': `["And some disagree and say in salting there is no distinction. Rather whether above or below — forever the salted imparts to brine and does not absorb from it; and the reason the poskim wrote: specifically in fire's boiling there is a distinction because through fire the overpowering one boils its fellow or cools it — for such is the way of fire's products; but through salting there is no power in the brine to make the salted brine, for salted is not like boiling until one salts on both sides and salts well well; and likewise brine has no power to make salted brine — as is said and one rinses well well; therefore with fire even if prohibition is cold the permitted is heated — unlike here; and even if prohibition is above, the lower has no power to make it brine — end of his words:"]`,
    '13#ב': `["Since they are. Meaning therefore permitted even l'chatchila to place in a prohibition vessel since they are dry; and so Bedek Malkhut; and Turei Zahav general rule 17 section 2 — but b'dieved even if moist it is permitted because there is no salting for vessels — as explained — see there in Turei Zahav:"]`,
    '3#ו': `["Forbidden to pour, etc. Because vapor rises from the pot upward — so is proved the reason Mordechai brought:"]`,
    '4#א': `["Thigh that was roasted, etc. Meaning gid fat forbids d'rabbanan as above siman 65 section 9 and siman 100 — whereas the gid body is only like wood; and the rabbi in Turei Zahav end of general rule 37 wrote thigh roasted with gid fat requires a peel — and it must be not specifically mentioned but in any case requires removal; and here he was general like the Mechaber's words — or here he holds nevertheless this is for the main law; but according to custom the rabbi's view is that where there is not slight loss even in such cases one requires sixty even in salting — all the more so in roasting:"]`,
    '4#ב': `["But if it is in gravy. For R' Yehudah even part of it like roasted — as explained above siman 92 section 2 and see there these laws:"]`,
    '5#א': `["And a permitted piece that was roasted, etc. The reason Beit Yosef wrote: the permitted goes and imparts taste to the prohibition and the prohibition returns and imparts to the permitted — see in s.k. 18:"]`,
    '5#ג': `["Or other prohibition to which fattiness applies. And it was explained in several places that blood does not have fattiness; nevertheless in section 9 it will be explained that we practice to estimate salting in sixty even in completely lean — if so the same in roasting, for roasting and salting are equal in this; and so it clearly appears in Turei Zahav end of general rule 37 and from Maharshal and other Acharonim; and that the rabbi was general here that a completely lean prohibition forbids only as much as removal — this is in d'rabbanan prohibition as below section 9 — see there:"]`,
    '8#א': `["And some say, etc. The reason Semak (siman 24 daf 95 side 1) wrote that what the Talmud says the membrane interrupts is not in the conclusion; and Maharai added another reason that we are concerned lest the membrane diminished:"]`,
    '8#ב': `["And if it was cooked thus, etc. It implies even if not salted or roasted first — nevertheless it is dabuk prohibition and requires sixty against the whole kulya; and we do not say the membrane interrupts so it should not absorb faster than other pieces; and so Maharai in his writings siman 98:"]`,
    '9#א': `["Salted that is not eaten because of its saltiness. And its measure of salting was already explained in siman 91 section 5 — see there:"]`,
    '9#ד': `["That the milk was dabuk. Or placed next to it; and see responsum of R' Shlomo Cohen part 2 siman 35:"]`,
    '9#ו': `["One in two nullifies. Meaning when there is in all three to nullify the milk itself — for otherwise it is not nullified by majority — lest they cook and impart taste; and as below siman 109:"]`,
    '9#ז': `["Even if they are chadichah na'aseh nevelah. Since the prohibition is not from themselves — as is said in siman 101:"]`,
    '9#ח': `["And some say all salting, etc. Meaning even in actual milk; and this is Ra'avyah's view in Mordechai and other poskim; and so Ramban whom Ran brought:"]`,
    '9#ט': `["And according to, etc. It refers above — meaning the primary view is the first reasoning that in fatty substance it forbids all; and because we are not expert we practice to estimate all salting in sixty, etc.; and he did not bring the Yesh Omrim except because he wants to conclude nevertheless one may rely in such a case on the lenient views, etc.:"]`,
    '9#י': `["And if he does not know which touched — all are permitted. Nullified by majority — Turei Zahav there; and it deals when it is known it did not touch the majority:"]`,
    '9#ל': `["Such as chametz on Pesach, etc. However in Orach Chayyim siman 467 section 14 the rabbi wrote one should forbid that whole piece on which a wheat of Pesach was found and permit the others by a peel; and in responsum 28 he expanded on this:"]`,
    '11#א': `["This that we said, etc. See above siman 70 s.k. 22:"]`,
    '13#א': `["Salt or spices, etc. permitted to put in milk. Also for the reason above; but the reason of nosen taam bar nosen taam does not permit, for the sharpness of salt and spices makes it as if in substance — as explained in siman 95 and see there; and see in siman 91 s.k. 2:"]`,
    '13#ג': `["Since they are. Meaning therefore permitted even l'chatchila to place in a prohibition vessel — as Tosafot explained in Avodah Zarah daf 32 side 1 s.v. and behold:"]`,
    '10#_': `And some forbid, etc. See above siman 70 s.k. 30.`,
    '12#_': `For there is no salted like boiling, etc. So all the poskim; and see siman 69 small section 2 and s.k. 87.`,
    '14#_': `If there is sixty against the salt. And sixty does not suffice against the blood absorbed in it, for we do not know how much blood was absorbed; and see siman 69 s.k. 33.`,
  },};
